<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Normals: where the normal meets the curve again (INNODEMS)</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>The graph shows the parabola \( y = {@fx@} \) and the point \( P \) on it where \( x = {@aa@} \).</p>
[[jsxgraph width="420px" height="400px"]]
var c = {#cq#}, a = {#float(aa)#}, x2 = {#float(x2)#};
function f(x){ return x*x + c; }
var R = Math.max(Math.abs(a), Math.abs(x2)) + 1.5;
var top = Math.max(f(R), f(a)) + 1, bot = c - 0.15*(top - c) - 1;
var board = JXG.JSXGraph.initBoard(divid, {
    boundingbox: [-R, top, R, bot],
    axis: true, showCopyright: false, showNavigation: false,
    pan: {enabled:false}, zoom: {enabled:false}
});
board.create('functiongraph', [f, -R, R], {strokeColor:'#1565c0', strokeWidth:2, highlight:false});
board.create('point', [a, f(a)], {name:'P', size:3, fixed:true, fillColor:'#d32f2f', strokeColor:'#d32f2f', label:{offset:[8, 8]}});
[[/jsxgraph]]
<p>The normal to the parabola at \( P \) crosses the parabola again at a second point \( Q \).</p>
<p>(a) Find the equation of the normal at \( P \).</p>
<div>\( y = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</div>
<div>[[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p>(b) Find the coordinates of \( Q \). Give exact values (fractions are fine).</p>
<div>\( x = \) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>
<div>\( y = \) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>(a)</strong> \( \dfrac{dy}{dx} = 2x \), so the tangent at \( P \) has gradient \( 2({@aa@}) = {@2*aa@} \) and the normal has gradient \( {@mn@} \). The point is \( P({@aa@},\ {@ya@}) \), so</p>
\[ y = {@mn@}\left(x - ({@aa@})\right) + {@ya@} = {@nl@}. \]
<p><strong>(b)</strong> Solve the normal and the parabola simultaneously:</p>
\[
\begin{align*}
{@fx@} &= {@nl@} \\
{@expand(fx - nl)@} &= 0.
\end{align*}
\]
<p>We already know one solution: \( x = {@aa@} \) (the point \( P \)), so \( x - ({@aa@}) \) is a factor:</p>
\[ {@factor(expand(fx - nl))@} = 0. \]
<p>The other solution is \( x = {@x2@} \). Then \( y = \left({@x2@}\right)^2 + ({@cq@}) = {@y2@} \), so \( Q = \left({@x2@},\ {@y2@}\right) \).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
